// ScalePerType — the `--per-type` pass: not "how much did the compiler allocate", but WHAT did it
// allocate, and WHERE FROM.
//
// The main run answers the first question. When its numbers move — when a phase's allocations start
// growing faster than the program does, or simply when a rung's exact count shifts — the next
// question is immediately "of WHAT?", and the measured binary cannot answer it: its counters are a
// single running total, deliberately, because anything per-type on that path would be a
// per-allocation write to a per-type slot and would perturb the very thing being measured.
//
// TWO TABLES, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE THAT FINDS THINGS. Every trace line carries both the type
// allocated and the SCOPE that allocated it, so both are tallied:
//
//   BY TYPE   names the DATA STRUCTURE behind a movement. A `LiveIndexColumn` that QUADRUPLES while
//             the program doubles is a quadratic, and the type is enough to find it.
//   BY SCOPE  names the CODE. And the type table structurally cannot: a `String` row says a String
//             was allocated: it can never say that 150 of them came from `emitFixedToken`,
//             materializing the same `"("` on every token, because they are indistinguishable from
//             every other String in the compiler. A constant-factor hog HIDES INSIDE ITS TYPE.
//             It is a single named row here.
//
// Both tables carry the same growth reading the rest of the suite prints — the ratio of the top
// traced rung to the one below it, on a ladder that doubles — so a scope that grows superlinearly is
// as visible as a type that does.
//
// So this is a SECOND, UNTIMED PASS over the same ladder, through a SECOND, DIFFERENTLY-COMPILED
// binary — and both of those words are load-bearing:
//
//   DIFFERENTLY-COMPILED  `--mm-trace` is a codegen flag. The binary it produces emits a line per
//                         allocation carrying the TYPE and the SIZE. It is not the binary whose time
//                         was measured and its timings mean nothing; only its allocation SHAPE does.
//   SECOND                It cannot be the binary under test, and it cannot even overwrite it: the
//                         C# driver has no output override, so building shv2 with --mm-trace would
//                         write over `maxon-shv2.exe` — the very executable running this code, which
//                         Windows locks anyway. So the sources are COPIED to `.scale-tmp/` and the
//                         trace binary is built there, beside the corpus it will compile.
//
// BOUNDED ON PURPOSE, AND IT SAYS SO. The top rung allocates ~37 million times, and tracing writes a
// line for each: gigabytes of pipe traffic to answer a question the bottom rungs already answer. So
// this covers the BOTTOM THREE RUNGS — enough for a growth reading (the top traced rung over the one
// below it), which is the whole point: a type whose count QUADRUPLES while the program doubles is the
// smoking gun. The runner PRINTS what it skipped: a silent cap reads as "we covered everything" when
// it did not.
//
// The trace is STREAMED, never collected. Rung 2 alone is ~1.7 million events; buffering that as a
// String would cost more memory than the compile it is measuring. `StreamingSubprocess.readStderrLine`
// hands them over one at a time and they are folded into the per-type table as they arrive.

// The three rungs this pass covers. See the header: bounded, and the bound is announced.
let PerTypeRungCount = 3

// How many rows of the ranked table to print. The tail of a per-type table is a long list of ones.
let PerTypeTopRows = 20

// Where the trace binary's sources are copied to, and where it is built.
let TraceSourceDirName = "mmtrace-src"
let ShvSourceDirName = "maxon-shv2"

// The trace stream's own vocabulary (see RuntimeEmitter's `__mm_tag_alloc`). A traced allocation is
//     mm_alloc <TypeName> #<id> size=<bytes> [<scope>]
// and a raw buffer — the header-free storage behind an array or a string — has no type at all:
//     mm_raw_alloc #R<id> size=<bytes> [<scope>]
let TraceAllocTag = "mm_alloc"
let TraceRawAllocTag = "mm_raw_alloc"
let TraceSizePrefix = "size="
let TraceScopeOpen = 0x5B as Byte  // '['
let TraceScopeClose = 0x5D as Byte  // ']'

// Printable-ASCII bounds, for the scope field's sanity check — see `traceLineScope`.
let AsciiPrintableLow = 0x20 as Byte
let AsciiPrintableHigh = 0x7E as Byte

// A scope field this parser could not read: absent, empty, or a shape it does not know. Counted
// under a name that says so rather than dropped, so the two tables always reconcile to the same
// total.
let UnknownScopeName = "(no scope)"

// The KNOWN corrupt-scope line. `--mm-trace` takes the scope from a C string handed to the runtime
// at the allocation site, and roughly one line in four million arrives with binary garbage in that
// field (see `foldTraceLine`'s header). Those allocations are real and are counted — but under this
// name, never decoded and never printed, because the bytes are not text.
let CorruptScopeName = "(corrupt scope tag)"

// Raw buffers are counted under one name rather than dropped. They carry most of the BYTE volume
// (array elements, string bytes), so a table that omitted them would explain a byte-volume
// regression as coming from nowhere. They have no type, and this name says so rather than inventing
// one.
let RawBufferTypeName = "(raw buffer)"

// One KEY's allocation profile across the traced rungs — a key being a type name in one table and
// an allocating scope in the other. `counts` and `bytes` are parallel with the rungs traced, so the
// growth reading is the same rung-over-rung division every other table in this suite takes.
//
// Nothing here is type-specific, and that is deliberate: the scope table is this same machinery
// keyed differently, not a second copy of it.
type AllocProfile
	export var name as String
	export var counts as AllocCountArray
	export var bytes as ByteCountArray

	export static function create(name String, rungs RungCount) returns AllocProfile
		var counts = AllocCountArray.create()
		var bytes = ByteCountArray.create()
		counts.resize(rungs)
		bytes.resize(rungs)
		return Self{name: name, counts: counts, bytes: bytes}
	end 'create'

	export function add(rung RungIndex, size ByteCount)
		let count = try self.counts.get(rung) otherwise panic("AllocProfile.add: rung {rung} outside the traced range")
		try self.counts.set(rung, value: count + 1) otherwise panic("AllocProfile.add: counts.set OOB at rung {rung}")

		let volume = try self.bytes.get(rung) otherwise panic("AllocProfile.add: rung {rung} outside the traced range")
		try self.bytes.set(rung, value: volume + size) otherwise panic("AllocProfile.add: bytes.set OOB at rung {rung}")
	end 'add'

	// This type's total allocations across every traced rung — the ranking key.
	export function totalCount() returns AllocCount
		var total = 0 as AllocCount
		for count in self.counts 'each'
			total = total + count
		end 'each'
		return total
	end 'totalCount'

	export function countAt(rung RungIndex) returns AllocCount
		return try self.counts.get(rung) otherwise panic("AllocProfile.countAt: rung {rung} outside the traced range")
	end 'countAt'
end 'AllocProfile'

typealias AllocProfileArray = Array with AllocProfile
typealias ProfileIndexMap = Map with (String, ProfileIndex)
typealias ProfileIndex = int(0 to 65535)

// A table of profiles keyed by name, accumulated across the traced rungs. TWO are built from the
// same stream: one keyed by the TYPE allocated, one by the SCOPE that allocated it.
type AllocProfileTable
	// What this table's keys ARE ("type" / "scope"), for the report's column header. The table is
	// key-agnostic; only the heading knows which question it answers.
	export var keyHeading as String
	export var profiles as AllocProfileArray
	export var indexByName as ProfileIndexMap
	export var tracedRungs as RungCount

	export static function create(keyHeading String, tracedRungs RungCount) returns AllocProfileTable
		return Self{keyHeading: keyHeading, profiles: AllocProfileArray.create(), indexByName: ProfileIndexMap.create(), tracedRungs: tracedRungs}
	end 'create'

	export function record(key String, rung RungIndex, size ByteCount)
		let index = try self.indexByName.get(key) otherwise 'newKey'
			let fresh = self.profiles.count() as ProfileIndex
			var profile = AllocProfile.create(key, rungs: self.tracedRungs)
			profile.add(rung, size: size)
			self.profiles.push(profile)
			self.indexByName.upsert(key, value: fresh)
			return
		end 'newKey'

		var profile = try self.profiles.get(index) otherwise panic("AllocProfileTable.record: index {index} for '{key}' is not in profiles")
		profile.add(rung, size: size)
	end 'record'

	// Every traced allocation, summed over every key — the reconciliation total.
	export function totalCount() returns AllocCount
		var total = 0 as AllocCount
		for profile in self.profiles 'each'
			total = total + profile.totalCount()
		end 'each'
		return total
	end 'totalCount'
end 'AllocProfileTable'

// What each table's keys are, and how wide to print them. A scope is a fully-qualified function
// name (`Compiler.Targets.Shared.collectPhiEdgeHints`) and needs far more room than a type name.
let TypeKeyHeading = "type"
let TypeKeyWidth = 28 as ColumnWidth
let ScopeKeyHeading = "scope (the function that allocated)"
let ScopeKeyWidth = 44 as ColumnWidth

// The two tables the ONE stream feeds, and the reason the scope table is worth having:
//
//   BY TYPE   answers "what was allocated" — a quadratic `LiveIndexColumn` names the data structure
//             behind a memory number that moved.
//   BY SCOPE  answers "which code allocated it" — and the type table structurally CANNOT. A `String`
//             row says a String was allocated; it cannot say that 150 of them came from
//             `emitFixedToken`, materializing the same `"("` on every token. Every constant-factor
//             win in the compiler's memory has been found in this column, never the other one.
//
// Both are written at the SAME point from the same line (`record` below is the only writer), so they
// cannot drift apart: every allocation is counted exactly once in each, and their totals must be
// equal. `reconcile` checks that rather than assuming it.
type TraceTables
	export var byType as AllocProfileTable
	export var byScope as AllocProfileTable

	export static function create(tracedRungs RungCount) returns TraceTables
		return Self{
			byType: AllocProfileTable.create(TypeKeyHeading, tracedRungs: tracedRungs),
			byScope: AllocProfileTable.create(ScopeKeyHeading, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)
		}
	end 'create'

	export function record(typeName String, scope String, rung RungIndex, size ByteCount)
		self.byType.record(typeName, rung: rung, size: size)
		self.byScope.record(scope, rung: rung, size: size)
	end 'record'
end 'TraceTables'

// ===========================================================================================
// Entry point
// ===========================================================================================

// Run the per-type pass over the bottom rungs of `results`.
//
// Its numbers are NOT the main run's numbers and are never compared with them: the trace binary is
// different codegen, built from a copy of the sources at a different path, so its counts are close to
// but not identical with the ones the trend log records. What it is FOR is naming the type — and the
// function — behind a movement the main run has already shown you.
// ⭐⭐ **IT TAKES THE TRACE COMPILER RATHER THAN BUILDING ONE, AND THAT IS WHERE THE PASS WAS DEAD.**
// The trace binary can only be built by the BOOTSTRAP, and the bootstrap takes this checkout's tree
// lock for EVERY directory build — it has no `-o` and so no way to say "the caller owns this output"
// (`maxon-sharp/Program.cs`, the `A PROJECT BUILD IS TREE-LEVEL` arm). `scale-test` holds that same
// lock for its whole run, so the child was refused, this pass printed `SKIPPED`, and the run exited 0.
// It could not have run ONCE since the lock landed. `runScaleTest` now builds the compiler BEFORE it
// takes the lock and hands it in; see there for why that shape and not re-entrancy.
//
// ⚠ AND FAILURE IS NO LONGER A SKIP. A pass that was ASKED for and did not produce its tables did not
// measure anything, which is what `brokenRun` means everywhere else in this instrument. The exit code
// is returned rather than swallowed.
export function runPerTypePass(results RungResultArray, traceExe FilePath, corpusRoot FilePath) returns ExitCode
	print("\n")
	print("scale-test: --per-type — tracing WHAT was allocated (second binary).\n")

	let tracedRungs = perTypeRungCount(results)
	if tracedRungs < results.count() 'bounded'
		// SAY WHAT WAS SKIPPED. The top rung allocates tens of millions of times and tracing it
		// would write gigabytes; that is a deliberate bound, and a bound nobody is told about reads
		// as complete coverage.
		print("  BOUNDED: tracing rungs 0-{tracedRungs - 1} of {results.count()}. The upper rungs allocate tens of millions\n")
		print("           of times and a trace line is written for each; the bottom rungs already give the\n")
		print("           per-type growth reading, which is what this pass exists to produce.\n")
	end 'bounded'

	var tables = TraceTables.create(tracedRungs)
	for rung in 0 upto tracedRungs 'eachRung'
		print("  tracing rung {rung}...\n")
		try traceRung(traceExe, rung: rung as RungIndex, tables: tables, corpusRoot: corpusRoot) otherwise (error) 'traceFailed'
			return brokenRun("--per-type was requested and rung {rung} could not be traced", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'traceFailed'
	end 'eachRung'

	reportPerType(tables, results: results, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)
	return 0
end 'runPerTypePass'

function perTypeRungCount(results RungResultArray) returns RungCount
	if results.count() < PerTypeRungCount 'fewer'
		return results.count() as RungCount
	end 'fewer'
	return PerTypeRungCount
end 'perTypeRungCount'

// ===========================================================================================
// Building the trace compiler
// ===========================================================================================

// Copy shv2's sources beside the corpus and build them with `--mm-trace`.
//
// A COPY, not an in-place rebuild, and that is not fastidiousness: `bin/maxon build maxon-shv2` has
// no output override, so it would write over `maxon-shv2/.maxon/maxon-shv2.exe` — the executable
// this code is running from. On Windows the OS locks it and the build simply fails; on a platform
// that permitted it, the suite would have silently replaced the binary under test with a traced one
// halfway through its own run.
// ⚠ **IT MUST BE CALLED BEFORE `scale-test` TAKES THE TREE LOCK.** The spawn below is a BOOTSTRAP
// directory build, and the bootstrap locks the checkout for every one of those unconditionally — there
// is no `-o` on that side to say the caller owns the output, which is the escape every other nested
// build in this harness uses. Called from inside the locked run it is refused, every time.
export function buildTraceCompiler(corpusRoot FilePath) returns FilePath throws ScaleError
	let bootstrap = try findBootstrapCompiler()
	let sourceDir = try FilePath.from(ShvSourceDirName) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{ShvSourceDirName}' is not a valid path")
	let traceDir = try corpusChild(corpusRoot, name: TraceSourceDirName)

	print("  copying shv2 sources to {traceDir.toString()} and building with --mm-trace...\n")
	try copyMaxonTree(sourceDir, into: traceDir)

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(BuildSubcommandName)
	argv.push(traceDir.toString())
	argv.push("--mm-trace")

	let build = try runProcess(Executable.path(bootstrap), argv: argv, workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not spawn the C# bootstrap to build a trace compiler: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	if build.exitCode != 0 'buildFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("building the --mm-trace compiler failed (exit {build.exitCode}): {buildFailureReason(build)}")
	end 'buildFailed'

	return try findBuiltExe(traceDir)
end 'buildTraceCompiler'

// Why the trace build failed, in the words the child actually used.
//
// ⚠ **STDERR ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE SABOTAGE THAT PROVED THIS PASS NOW REFUSES IS ALSO WHAT
// FOUND IT.** Handing the bootstrap a flag it does not know makes it print its usage to STDOUT and exit
// non-zero, so a refusal quoting only `stderr` read `failed (exit 1): ` — loud, correctly refusing, and
// silent about why. A reason a reader cannot act on is most of the way back to the `SKIPPED` this whole
// change removes, so the fallback is not decoration.
function buildFailureReason(build ProcessCapture) returns String
	let reason = build.stderr.trim()
	if not reason.isEmpty() 'saidSomethingOnStderr'
		return reason
	end 'saidSomethingOnStderr'

	let fallback = build.stdout.trim()
	if fallback.isEmpty() 'saidNothingAtAll'
		return "it wrote nothing to stderr or stdout"
	end 'saidNothingAtAll'
	return fallback
end 'buildFailureReason'

// The C# bootstrap, found by walking up from this compiler's own location. The trace binary can only
// be built by the bootstrap — shv2 cannot compile itself — so its absence is a clean SKIP with a
// reason, never a crash.
function findBootstrapCompiler() returns FilePath throws ScaleError
	let relative = try FilePath.from("bin/maxon{hostExecutableExtension()}") otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("the bootstrap path is not a valid relative path")
	let candidate = Directory.currentPath().join(relative)
	if not File.exists(candidate) 'missing'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the C# bootstrap is not at {candidate.toString()}, and only it can build a --mm-trace compiler. Build it first (`dotnet build` in maxon-sharp), and run scale-test from the repo root.")
	end 'missing'
	return candidate
end 'findBootstrapCompiler'

// The `.maxon/<project>.exe` a directory build produces. Located by SCANNING rather than by
// reconstructing the name: the driver derives it from the project's `build.maxon`, and a second copy
// of that naming rule here would be a second thing to keep in step with it.
function findBuiltExe(traceDir FilePath) returns FilePath throws ScaleError
	let outDirName = try FilePath.from(".maxon") otherwise panic("findBuiltExe: '.maxon' must be a valid relative path")
	let outDir = traceDir.join(outDirName)

	let entries = try Directory.list(outDir) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("the --mm-trace build produced no {outDir.toString()} directory")

	// Asked ONCE. It was four calls in this loop, two of them re-asking the same question inside one
	// condition, and each answer is a fresh `String` the host's target model rebuilds per entry.
	let suffix = hostExecutableExtension()
	for entry in entries 'each'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'skipCache'
			continue
		end 'skipCache'
		if not suffix.isEmpty() and entry.filename().endsWith(suffix) 'isExe'
			return entry
		end 'isExe'
		if suffix.isEmpty() and not entry.filename().contains(".") 'isPosixExe'
			return entry
		end 'isPosixExe'
	end 'each'

	throw ScaleError.reason("the --mm-trace build produced no executable under {outDir.toString()}")
end 'findBuiltExe'

// Recursively copy every `.maxon` file (and `build.maxon`, which names the output) from `from` into
// `into`, preserving the directory structure.
//
// IT HONOURS `.maxonignore`, AND IT MUST. `maxon-shv2/track0/` is a SEPARATE PROGRAM that happens to
// live inside the compiler's tree — it has its own `main` and its own `StringArray` — and it is
// excluded from the build by a `.maxonignore` marker. A copy that took it along would produce a
// source tree with two `main`s and a clashing typealias, and the trace build would fail with a
// hundred errors that had nothing to do with tracing. (It did exactly that.)
//
// The rule is the compiler's own and is now CALLED rather than re-spelled: `Compiler.holdsIgnoreMarker`
// is the same predicate the source walk prunes with. A private copy of it lived here until the walk
// grew the clause, and a copy is only ever a second answer waiting to drift from the first.
//
// THE COPY IS A VALUE, NOT A PLACE — it is exactly the source tree, and `pruneCopiedDir` is what
// makes that true. `.scale-tmp/` is never cleaned, and this copy used only ever to WRITE: a source
// file DELETED from the compiler survived forever in a `mmtrace-src` left by an older run, and
// `build <dir>` walks recursively, so the trace compiler was built from the dead file too.
//
// That is not hypothetical and it did not fail loudly. Retiring the scale suite's gate apparatus
// deleted `Testing/ScaleGates.maxon`, `Testing/ScaleBaseline.maxon` and `Testing/CurveFit.maxon`
// (commit 3e34836a8) — and every machine with a `.scale-tmp` older than that commit kept compiling
// them, so the trace build died on `Unknown type: ScaleVerdict` and `--per-type` SKIPPED with a
// printed line and an exit code of 0. The by-SCOPE attribution table — the one thing that can say
// WHICH FUNCTION made an allocation — was silently unavailable on exactly the machines that had used
// the suite before, and available only on a fresh checkout. This is `pruneRungDir`'s bug, in the one
// directory `pruneRungDir` does not own.
function copyMaxonTree(from FilePath, into FilePath) throws ScaleError
	if not Directory.create(into) 'noDir'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create {into.toString()}")
	end 'noDir'

	// Every name this level of the source tree owns — directories recursed into AND files written.
	// Anything else found in `into` is residue from an older run and must not reach the build.
	var copied = StringArray.create()

	let entries = try Directory.list(from) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not list {from.toString()}")
	for entry in entries 'each'
		let name = entry.filename()

		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'isDir'
			// A leading dot is build output (`.maxon/`), not source; a `.maxonignore` marker means the
			// directory is deliberately not part of this project. Neither is `copied` — an ignored
			// directory that an OLDER copy took along is residue like any other, and the prune below
			// empties it.
			if name.startsWith(".") or holdsIgnoreMarker(entry) 'skipNonSource'
				continue
			end 'skipNonSource'
			let childName = try FilePath.from(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{name}' is not a valid path component")
			try copyMaxonTree(entry, into: into.join(childName))
			copied.push(name)
			continue
		end 'isDir'

		if not name.endsWith(MaxonExtension) 'notSource'
			continue
		end 'notSource'

		let content = try File.readText(entry) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not read {entry.toString()}")
		let childName = try FilePath.from(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{name}' is not a valid path component")
		try File.writeText(into.join(childName), content: content) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not write {into.join(childName).toString()}")
		copied.push(name)
	end 'each'

	try pruneCopiedDir(into, copied: copied)
end 'copyMaxonTree'

// Delete every `.maxon` file under `into` that the copy did not just write, so the tree the trace
// compiler is built from is exactly the tree the compiler is built from.
//
// ONLY `.maxon` FILES ARE DELETED, and only they need to be: `build <dir>` compiles nothing else, so
// a stray non-source file cannot change what the trace binary IS. (It would change what `loadProject`
// ENUMERATES — but this directory is the compiler being built, not the corpus being measured, so its
// own load cost is not a number this suite reports.)
//
// A stale SUBDIRECTORY is EMPTIED rather than removed. The stdlib has no recursive directory remove,
// and it does not need one here: a directory holding no `.maxon` file contributes nothing to a build
// that only ever compiles `.maxon` files, so an empty husk is as good as a deleted one and needs no
// mechanism that does not exist. Dot-directories are left entirely alone — `.maxon/` is the trace
// build's OWN output and cache, and reaching into it would be this function deleting the artifact it
// exists to produce.
function pruneCopiedDir(into FilePath, copied StringArray) throws ScaleError
	let entries = try Directory.list(into) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not list {into.toString()}")

	for entry in entries 'each'
		let name = entry.filename()
		if copied.contains(name) 'justCopied'
			continue
		end 'justCopied'

		if name.startsWith(".") 'buildOutput'
			continue
		end 'buildOutput'

		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'staleDir'
			try pruneCopiedDir(entry, copied: StringArray.create())
			continue
		end 'staleDir'

		if not name.endsWith(MaxonExtension) 'notCompiled'
			continue
		end 'notCompiled'

		try File.delete(entry) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not delete the stale source {entry.toString()} from the trace tree, and `build` would compile it as part of the trace compiler")
	end 'each'
end 'pruneCopiedDir'

// The root arrives as an argument rather than being rebuilt from `CorpusRelativeRoot`, which this file
// did at all three places it needed a corpus path. That is a DUPLICATION removal and nothing more.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A BUG FIX, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS COMMENT SAID IT WAS.** The obvious reading —
// "`--emit-corpus=<dir>` moves the rungs and this pass would go looking under `.scale-tmp`" — is
// WRONG, and it was written here before anyone checked: `measureScaleLadder` returns 0 the moment
// `--emit-corpus` is set (`scale-test: corpus written to … (--emit-corpus: not compiling)`), before a
// single rung is compiled and long before this pass is reached. The two flags cannot meet, so the
// hardcoded root was never once consulted for the wrong directory. Recorded rather than quietly
// corrected, because a cure named for a defect that does not exist is how the next reader inherits a
// diagnosis instead of the evidence.
function corpusChild(corpusRoot FilePath, name String) returns FilePath throws ScaleError
	let child = try FilePath.from(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{name}' is not a valid path component")
	return corpusRoot.join(child)
end 'corpusChild'

// ===========================================================================================
// Tracing one rung
// ===========================================================================================

// Compile one rung through the trace binary, folding its stderr into the per-type table as it
// arrives.
//
// STREAMED, NOT COLLECTED. Rung 2 emits ~1.7 million trace lines; buffering them would cost more
// memory than the compile being measured. Each line is folded in and dropped.
function traceRung(traceExe FilePath, rung RungIndex, tables TraceTables, corpusRoot FilePath) throws ScaleError
	let rungDirName = try FilePath.from("{RungDirPrefix}{rung}") otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung} has no valid directory name")
	let dir = corpusRoot.join(rungDirName)

	let outName = try FilePath.from("trace{rung}") otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung} has no valid output name")

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(BuildSubcommandName)
	argv.push(dir.toString())
	argv.push(OutputFlagName)
	argv.push(corpusRoot.join(outName).toString())

	var child = try StreamingSubprocess.spawn(Executable.path(traceExe), arguments: argv) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not spawn the trace compiler on rung {rung}: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	while true 'eachLine'
		let line = try child.readStderrLine() otherwise (e) 'readFailed'
			child.release()
			throw ScaleError.reason("reading the trace of rung {rung} failed: {e.displayReason()}")
		end 'readFailed'

		if line.isEmpty() 'endOfStream'
			break
		end 'endOfStream'

		foldTraceLine(line, rung: rung, tables: tables)
	end 'eachLine'

	let code = try child.wait() otherwise (e) 'waitFailed'
		child.release()
		throw ScaleError.reason("the trace compiler on rung {rung} could not be waited on: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'waitFailed'
	child.release()

	if code != 0 'compileFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("the trace compiler exited {code} on rung {rung}")
	end 'compileFailed'
end 'traceRung'

// Fold one line of the trace into the table, if it is an allocation at all. Everything else in the
// stream — frees, increfs, the slab and OS layers beneath — is skipped in silence: this pass asks
// one question, and the answer is in the `mm_alloc` and `mm_raw_alloc` lines.
//
// IT NEVER CALLS `trim`, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE — IT IS A FACT ABOUT THE STREAM. A trace line ends
// with the allocating scope, taken from a C string the runtime is handed at the allocation site, and
// ONE LINE IN FOUR MILLION arrives with a garbage scope:
//
//     mm_alloc (null) #586 size=336 [<5 bytes of binary>]
//
// That is not valid UTF-8. `trim` is grapheme-aware, so it DECODES, and it panics on that line —
// taking the whole run down over a corrupt byte in a diagnostic field this pass does not even read.
// (The garbage tag is a pre-existing defect in --mm-trace's scope plumbing. This pass cannot fix it
// and must not die of it.)
//
// `split` and `startsWith` compare bytes and do not decode, so leading indent is handled by dropping
// the empty fields a split produces rather than by trimming them away.
function foldTraceLine(line String, rung RungIndex, tables TraceTables)
	let fields = nonEmptyFields(line)
	let tag = try fields.get(0) otherwise 'blankLine'
		return
	end 'blankLine'

	// A raw buffer carries a scope like any other allocation — it is the array elements or string
	// bytes behind SOME call — so it joins the scope table under its own type name.
	if tag == TraceRawAllocTag 'rawAlloc'
		tables.record(RawBufferTypeName, scope: traceLineScope(line), rung: rung, size: traceLineSize(fields))
		return
	end 'rawAlloc'

	if tag != TraceAllocTag 'notAnAlloc'
		return
	end 'notAnAlloc'

	// `mm_alloc <TypeName> #<id> size=<bytes> [<scope>]`
	let typeName = try fields.get(1) otherwise 'noType'
		return
	end 'noType'
	tables.record(typeName, scope: traceLineScope(line), rung: rung, size: traceLineSize(fields))
end 'foldTraceLine'

// The `[<scope>]` tag: the function that MADE the allocation.
//
// FIRST `[` TO LAST `]`, not the last whitespace-delimited field — because the scope is not always
// one field. A raw slice buffer NESTS the origin inside its own tag:
//
//     mm_raw_alloc #R24 size=1 [slice.buf [stdlib.String.replace]]
//
// Taking the last field there yields `stdlib.String.replace]` — a stray bracket, and the same scope
// split across two bogus rows depending on which shape emitted it. Bracket to bracket keeps the tag
// whole, nesting and all, and treats `slice.buf [stdlib.String.replace]` as the one thing it is.
//
// Read as BYTES, never decoded — the same fact `foldTraceLine`'s header records, not a matter of
// taste. The scope comes from a C string handed to the runtime at the allocation site and roughly
// one line in four million arrives with binary garbage in it. Anything grapheme-aware — `trim`,
// `slice`, `replace` — DECODES, and panics on those bytes, taking the run down over a corrupt
// diagnostic field. So a scope that is not printable ASCII is counted under `CorruptScopeName`
// rather than carried into a map key and onto a terminal.
function traceLineScope(line String) returns String
	let bytes = line.toByteArray()
	let count = bytes.count()

	var open = count
	var i = 0
	while i < count 'findOpen'
		let b = try bytes.get(i) otherwise panic("traceLineScope: i < count but get({i}) failed")
		if b == TraceScopeOpen 'found'
			open = i
			break
		end 'found'
		i = i + 1
	end 'findOpen'

	if open >= count 'noScope'
		return UnknownScopeName
	end 'noScope'

	var close = count
	var j = count
	while j > open 'findClose'
		j = j - 1
		let b = try bytes.get(j) otherwise panic("traceLineScope: j < count but get({j}) failed")
		if b == TraceScopeClose 'found'
			close = j
			break
		end 'found'
	end 'findClose'

	// `close <= open + 1` is an empty tag (`[]`); `close >= count` is an unterminated one.
	if close >= count or close <= open + 1 'emptyOrUnterminated'
		return UnknownScopeName
	end 'emptyOrUnterminated'

	let inner = try bytes.slice(open + 1, endIndex: close) otherwise return UnknownScopeName
	if not isPrintableAscii(inner) 'corrupt'
		return CorruptScopeName
	end 'corrupt'

	return String.from(inner)
end 'traceLineScope'

// Printable ASCII throughout — the guard that keeps a corrupt scope tag out of a map key and off the
// terminal. Byte-wise on purpose: deciding whether bytes are text must not require decoding them.
function isPrintableAscii(bytes ByteArray) returns bool
	for b in bytes 'each'
		if b < AsciiPrintableLow or b > AsciiPrintableHigh 'notPrintable'
			return false
		end 'notPrintable'
	end 'each'
	return true
end 'isPrintableAscii'

// The line's space-separated fields with the empties dropped — which is what makes the trace's depth
// indentation harmless without a `trim`.
function nonEmptyFields(line String) returns StringArray
	var out = StringArray.create()
	for field in line.split(" ") 'each'
		if not field.isEmpty() 'keep'
			out.push(field)
		end 'keep'
	end 'each'
	return out
end 'nonEmptyFields'

// The `size=<bytes>` field. A line without a readable one contributes zero BYTES and still
// contributes its COUNT — the count is the signal this pass is built around, and dropping the whole
// allocation because its size field was malformed would lose the more important half.
function traceLineSize(fields StringArray) returns ByteCount
	for field in fields 'each'
		if field.startsWith(TraceSizePrefix) 'isSize'
			let size = try parseCount(field.replace(TraceSizePrefix, with: "")) otherwise 'malformed'
				return 0
			end 'malformed'
			return size
		end 'isSize'
	end 'each'
	return 0
end 'traceLineSize'

// ===========================================================================================
// Reporting
// ===========================================================================================

// BOTH tables, WHAT was allocated and then WHERE FROM. The second is not a nicety: a `String` row in
// the first says a String was allocated and can never say which of the hundreds of places that
// allocate Strings did it, so a constant-factor hog is INVISIBLE there — it hides among every other
// user of its type. In the scope table it is a single named row.
function reportPerType(tables TraceTables, results RungResultArray, tracedRungs RungCount)
	reportTable(tables.byType, keyWidth: TypeKeyWidth, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)
	reportTable(tables.byScope, keyWidth: ScopeKeyWidth, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)
	reconcile(tables, results: results, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)
end 'reportPerType'

// One ranked table. Key-agnostic — it prints whatever the table is keyed by, under that table's own
// heading — which is why there is one of these and not two.
function reportTable(table AllocProfileTable, keyWidth ColumnWidth, tracedRungs RungCount)
	print("\n")
	print("  {padRight(table.keyHeading, width: keyWidth)}{padLeft("allocs", width: 12)}{padLeft("bytes", width: 14)}{padLeft("x", width: 11)}\n")

	let ranked = rankByTotalCount(table)
	var shown = 0
	for profile in ranked 'each'
		if shown >= PerTypeTopRows 'enough'
			print("  ... and {ranked.count() - PerTypeTopRows} more\n")
			break
		end 'enough'
		printProfileRow(profile, keyWidth: keyWidth, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)
		shown = shown + 1
	end 'each'
end 'reportTable'

// One row, with its own growth across the traced rungs — the number this pass exists to produce. A
// key that QUADRUPLES while the program doubles is the smoking gun behind a movement the timed run
// showed you: in the type table it names the data structure, in the scope table the function.
//
// The ladder doubles, so the ratio between the top traced rung and the one below it IS the growth,
// and it is the same reading — the same `x2.00 is linear` — that every other table in this suite
// prints. It replaced a fitted exponent over all the traced rungs, which was a different unit for the
// same fact and one more thing for a reader to hold in their head.
function printProfileRow(profile AllocProfile, keyWidth ColumnWidth, tracedRungs RungCount)
	var totalBytes = 0 as ByteCount
	for value in profile.bytes 'eachByte'
		totalBytes = totalBytes + value
	end 'eachByte'

	print("  {padRight(profile.name, width: keyWidth)}{padLeft("{profile.totalCount()}", width: 12)}{padLeft("{totalBytes}", width: 14)}{padLeft(growthText(topTracedGrowth(profile, tracedRungs: tracedRungs)), width: 11)}\n")
end 'printProfileRow'

// This key's growth at the TOP of the traced range — the steepest end, and the one a key that has gone
// superlinear shows it in first. Only the bottom rungs are traced (see `perTypeRungCount`), so this is
// the top of what was traced, not the top of the ladder.
function topTracedGrowth(profile AllocProfile, tracedRungs RungCount) returns GrowthReading
	if tracedRungs < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	let top = (tracedRungs - 1) as RungIndex
	let below = (tracedRungs - 2) as RungIndex
	return growthOf(profile.countAt(top), previous: profile.countAt(below))
end 'topTracedGrowth'

// Rank by total allocations, descending. A selection sort: the table has a few dozen types, and a
// real sort would be more machinery than the thing it sorts.
function rankByTotalCount(table AllocProfileTable) returns AllocProfileArray
	var remaining = AllocProfileArray.create()
	for profile in table.profiles 'each'
		remaining.push(profile)
	end 'each'

	var ranked = AllocProfileArray.create()
	while remaining.count() > 0 'pickLargest'
		var bestIndex = 0
		var best = try remaining.get(0) otherwise panic("rankByTotalCount: remaining is non-empty but get(0) failed")
		for i in 1 upto remaining.count() 'scan'
			let candidate = try remaining.get(i) otherwise panic("rankByTotalCount: remaining.get OOB at {i}")
			if candidate.totalCount() > best.totalCount() 'larger'
				best = candidate
				bestIndex = i
			end 'larger'
		end 'scan'

		ranked.push(best)

		// Compact by moving the tail into the hole — the order of `remaining` does not matter, so a
		// swap-with-last is enough and no element has to shift. `pop` returns the element it removed,
		// which is exactly the tail we want to move; taking it that way also avoids holding a borrow
		// of `remaining` across the `set` that writes back into it.
		let last = try remaining.pop() otherwise panic("rankByTotalCount: pop on a non-empty array failed")
		if bestIndex < remaining.count() 'notTheTail'
			try remaining.set(bestIndex, value: last) otherwise panic("rankByTotalCount: remaining.set OOB at {bestIndex}")
		end 'notTheTail'
	end 'pickLargest'
	return ranked
end 'rankByTotalCount'

// THE RECONCILIATION. Same spirit as `unattributedNanos`' panic: a number that does not add up is
// reported, not smoothed over.
//
// The trace covers the whole PROCESS — startup, argument parsing, the compile, teardown — while the
// timed binary's `total` covers `compile()` alone. So the traced count is expected to be LARGER, by
// whatever the process spends outside the compile. What must NEVER happen is the traced count coming
// in SMALLER: every allocation the compile made emitted a line, so a shortfall means lines were
// dropped — a parser that skipped a shape it did not recognise, or a stream that was truncated — and
// every per-type number above would be understated by an unknown amount.
function reconcile(tables TraceTables, results RungResultArray, tracedRungs RungCount)
	var timedTotal = 0 as AllocCount
	for rung in 0 upto tracedRungs 'eachRung'
		let result = try results.get(rung) otherwise panic("reconcile: results.get OOB at rung {rung}")
		timedTotal = timedTotal + result.total.allocs
	end 'eachRung'

	// The two tables are written at ONE point from ONE line (`TraceTables.record`), so they must hold
	// the same number of allocations. If they do not, the scope parse dropped or double-counted events
	// the type parse did not, and every row of the scope table is wrong by an unknown amount. Checked,
	// not assumed — the same rule the reconciliation below applies to the trace as a whole.
	let tracedTotal = tables.byType.totalCount()
	let scopeTotal = tables.byScope.totalCount()
	if scopeTotal != tracedTotal 'tablesDisagree'
		panic("reconcile: the scope table holds {scopeTotal} allocations but the type table holds {tracedTotal} — both are written by TraceTables.record, from the same line, so they cannot differ. The scope parse is dropping or double-counting events.")
	end 'tablesDisagree'

	print("\n")
	print("  reconciliation: {tracedTotal} traced allocation(s) vs {timedTotal} the timed binary attributed to the compile.\n")

	if tracedTotal < timedTotal 'lostEvents'
		printError("  RECONCILIATION FAILED: the trace accounts for FEWER allocations than the compile made.\n")
		printError("  Every allocation emits a trace line, so a shortfall means lines were DROPPED — and every\n")
		printError("  per-type number above is understated by an unknown amount. Do not trust this table.\n")
		return
	end 'lostEvents'

	// The excess is the process outside the compile: stdlib init, argument parsing, teardown. It is
	// the same handful of thousands at every rung, so it shrinks to nothing as a share as the ladder
	// climbs — which is itself a check that it is what it claims to be.
	print("  The {tracedTotal - timedTotal} extra are the process OUTSIDE the compile (startup, arg parsing,\n")
	print("  teardown) — the trace spans the whole process, the timed total spans compile() alone.\n")
	print("  NOTE: this is a --mm-trace binary built from a COPY of the sources. Different codegen, and\n")
	print("  different source paths, so its counts are close to but not identical with the timed run's.\n")
end 'reconcile'
